<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sea Peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1177 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century-bce">12th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sea Peoples were migrating confederacies of seafaring raiders who attacked the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a>. Egyptian records describe <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramesses-iii">Ramesses III</a> defeating them in land and sea battles around 1177 BCE. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> identify them as immediate agents of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> collapse that destroyed the Hittite state, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> survived in weakened form.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sea-peoples.jpg" alt="Sea Peoples" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artistUnknown artist in pay of Ramesses III · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Medinet_Habu_Ramses_III._Tempel_Nordostwand_Abzeichnung_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's final military test came under Ramesses III, who repelled land and sea invasions by the <b>Sea Peoples</b> around 1177 BCE, preserving Egypt while almost every other Bronze Age power collapsed or contracted.</blockquote><a href="/empire/egyptian-empire-new-kingdom">Egyptian Empire New Kingdom · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Sea Peoples</b>, migrating confederacies whose raids disrupted the coastal trade networks on which the Late Bronze Age international system depended, are traditionally cast as the immediate agents, though their exact role remains debated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…names and place the Phrygians' entry just before or after the collapse of the Hittite Empire at the start of the 12th century BCE, perhaps among the '<b>Sea Peoples</b>'; others believe they were already settled on the Sakarya in the Late Bronze Age among the Hittites' western subjects.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-phrygia">Kingdom of Phrygia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Shortly afterwards, around 1185 BCE, the city was sacked and burned, probably by seaborne raiders of the kind Egyptian sources call the <b>Sea Peoples</b>, although famine, earthquakes and the collapse of the Hittite state may all have contributed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ugarit">Kingdom of Ugarit · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the period, Egyptian inscriptions of Merneptah and Ramesses III list among the <b>Sea Peoples</b> groups such as the Ekwesh and the Denyen, whom some scholars connect with the Achaeans and the Danaans.</blockquote><a href="/culture/mycenaean-civilisation">Mycenaean civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Giovanni Ugas and other scholars identify the Sherden, one of the <b>Sea Peoples</b> of Egyptian sources, with the Nuragic Sardinians; others think the Sherden came to the island after failing to invade Egypt, and both views remain controversial.</blockquote><a href="/culture/nuragic-civilisation">Nuragic civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
